WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.760
Thanks everyone for hanging out today.

00:01.760 --> 00:05.160
I know it's been a long day, but hopefully you found this useful.

00:05.160 --> 00:08.560
As far as next steps go, there's a whole world

00:08.560 --> 00:11.600
of the types throughout there that we haven't even began to touch.

00:11.600 --> 00:15.120
Like I said, there are interfaces that you need to think about.

00:15.120 --> 00:18.800
There are generics and all these other things that you could do with TypeScript.

00:18.800 --> 00:21.600
But to be honest, my recommendation when it comes to TypeScript

00:21.600 --> 00:23.920
is similar to my advice when it comes to learning frameworks,

00:23.920 --> 00:26.640
which is, don't try to learn every single technique in the world

00:26.640 --> 00:28.960
in an attempt to like create the perfect code.

00:28.960 --> 00:31.040
When you have a specific problem that you have,

00:31.040 --> 00:33.040
that's when you reach out and find a solution for it.

00:33.040 --> 00:36.720
So a great example of that is when I realized that we had a constant

00:36.720 --> 00:40.560
that was keeping a source of truth for like our diet list and that kind of stuff.

00:40.560 --> 00:43.680
And then I realized that the TypeScript was replicating that in the types.

00:43.680 --> 00:47.040
This is when I went on to learn which admit it again.

00:47.040 --> 00:49.920
Autism tax, but it does solve a problem for me.

00:49.920 --> 00:51.760
And that's when I reached out for it.

00:51.760 --> 00:55.360
It's really tempting, I think, as developers to try to learn everything at once

00:55.360 --> 00:57.120
because you're afraid you'll miss something.

00:57.120 --> 01:00.720
But honestly, I think perfection is the enemy of progress

01:00.720 --> 01:03.840
and code purity can really get in the way of team progress

01:03.840 --> 01:07.360
when it comes to really what's most important, right?

01:07.360 --> 01:12.240
And so again, I can't stress this enough TypeScript like many tools.

01:12.240 --> 01:13.520
It's wonderful in many ways, right?

01:13.520 --> 01:14.800
We saw it do a lot of good things for us.

01:14.800 --> 01:17.120
But don't forget that we did that in a progressive way.

01:17.120 --> 01:20.320
That was like, let me help myself out by setting my intentions

01:20.320 --> 01:23.920
and then just like picking it out when it makes sense for me.

01:23.920 --> 01:26.320
But you'll notice there are sometimes I didn't define types either.

01:26.320 --> 01:27.040
And that's okay.

01:27.040 --> 01:29.040
That's actually a great way to just get your feet wet

01:29.040 --> 01:30.800
and figure out what makes the most sense.

01:30.800 --> 01:33.440
The moment you get caught up in abstraction, that kind of stuff,

01:33.440 --> 01:36.720
that's when it can just blow up in your face, then instead of shipping useful features,

01:36.720 --> 01:38.640
the customer is just sitting there fighting types.

01:38.640 --> 01:40.560
And I don't know, that's probably not the best use of our time, right?

01:40.560 --> 01:42.560
At the end of the day.

01:42.560 --> 01:46.400
Because if you think about it, this is what, this is what users see, right?

01:46.400 --> 01:48.720
When they get your code, they don't see beautiful types.

01:48.720 --> 01:49.600
They don't see all that stuff.

01:49.600 --> 01:50.560
That's for us.

01:50.560 --> 01:53.120
That's for us to help it maintain and to ship faster.

01:53.200 --> 01:57.680
But at the end of the day, it's all about the, it's all about the features that we ship.

01:57.680 --> 02:03.520
And I don't, you know, don't lose sight of that when working on code.

02:03.520 --> 02:06.160
With that, thank you, everyone, for joining me again today.

02:06.880 --> 02:08.400
It's been enough to loop blast.

