Yep.

02:11.600 --> 02:12.240
Go for it.

02:12.240 --> 02:17.360
Is there a litmus test to know when it's better to migrate to TypeScript,

02:17.360 --> 02:19.120
for scaling an app for the future?

02:19.120 --> 02:19.840
Great.

02:19.840 --> 02:20.320
Okay, yes.

02:20.320 --> 02:21.280
Thank you for the reminder.

02:21.280 --> 02:22.720
Okay, so this is where Shiva.

02:23.680 --> 02:26.320
When it comes to really thinking about scalability of code,

02:27.440 --> 02:29.920
basically, in my opinion, when it comes to the scale of code,

02:29.920 --> 02:33.520
it's about the ability of people to, how quickly someone can understand your code base.

02:34.080 --> 02:38.720
And that really sets apart something that scalable and maintainable versus something that is,

02:38.720 --> 02:43.200
is, like, basically tricky to scale at an enterprise level.

02:43.520 --> 02:47.120
And so TypeScript is one of those things, once again, that if your team is ready to start

02:47.200 --> 02:53.360
bringing that in and incorporating it, and in a way that doesn't halt the progress of the tool chain,

02:53.360 --> 02:55.600
then by all means, it certainly is useful for that.

02:55.600 --> 03:01.200
But again, there's so many real world scenarios where people tried to migrate,

03:01.200 --> 03:05.840
and they found themselves stuck in bill chain tools, like errors constantly.

03:05.840 --> 03:08.240
And so rather than making progress, they're just fighting over it.

03:08.240 --> 03:10.080
And then no one wants to touch any of the files.

03:10.080 --> 03:14.480
And then at the end of the day, you might be using an enterprise grade, like TypeScript,

03:14.480 --> 03:19.600
but if it's, you know, it's hindering progress, not a good thing at all.

03:19.600 --> 03:22.880
So once again, it's all about comprehension and ease of maintenance,

03:22.880 --> 03:25.920
as long as you get your team to feel comfortable with that,

03:25.920 --> 03:28.640
even if you just use JS docs, you don't have to use TypeScript, that works too.

03:29.520 --> 03:32.640
So I think that's really the key thing to focus on, and not the TypeScript part.

03:32.640 --> 03:36.720
Which is better for bigger projects, options API, or composition API?

03:36.720 --> 03:41.680
Yeah. So when it comes to using V3, I do think that at the end of the day,

03:41.680 --> 03:43.920
it's really hard to escape from composition API.

03:43.920 --> 03:46.320
We'll see this a lot in the next three workshop upcoming,

03:46.320 --> 03:48.480
but because of how modular it is,

03:48.480 --> 03:52.160
composition API will be a part of basically every V3 project going forward.

03:52.160 --> 03:55.920
That said, that doesn't mean that what you're doing with options API is out of date either.

03:55.920 --> 03:58.480
Because there are sometimes some components benefit from that structure,

03:58.480 --> 04:01.440
that options API provides, and so it's one of those things where

04:01.440 --> 04:04.160
choose the tool that best suits the problem that you're trying to solve,

04:04.160 --> 04:07.680
rather than trying to be like, I'm only going to use this one method.

04:07.680 --> 04:10.640
So understanding the trade-offs and knowing your team's abilities,

04:10.640 --> 04:12.640
that will be the key to success for your project.

